tion, leading to an increased sense of urgency.\\n\\nAdditionally, the fact that she doesn't leak at night suggests that her bladder doesn't fill up to a point where it's likely to leak during sleep, which is typical in cases of nocturnal enuresis. So, it's more likely related to the stress from activities like coughing rather than a condition that affects nighttime.\\n\\nIn terms of treatment, if cystometry shows a high residual volume and detrusor contractions, the next steps might include behavioral modifications, such as bladder training to improve sphincter control, and possibly medications to relax the bladder or reduce detrusor activity.\\n\\nSo, putting it all together, cystometry would likely reveal that her residual volume is increased, and there are detrusor contractions contributing to her involuntary urine loss during activities.\\n</think>\\n\\nBased on the patient's history and the findings from the gynecological exam and Q-tip test, cystometry would most likely reveal an increased residual volume in the bladder and detrusor contractions. This is consistent with the presentation of stress urinary incontinence, where involuntary bladder emptying occurs due to increased intra-abdominal pressure during activities like coughing. The Q-tip test likely confirmed normal urethral closure, supporting the diagnosis of stress incontinence rather than sphincter deficiency. Cystometry would help quantify these findings, guiding appropriate management strategies such as bladder training and possibly medication to address detrusor overactivity.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>\"]\n"
